              "content": "( \\mathrm { M g ^ { 2 + } } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . We use brackets ( ) when we have two or more polyatomic ions of the same type in a formula. In the example of aluminium hydroxide given below, the bracket around OH with a subscript 3 indicates that there are three hydroxide (OH–) ions bound to one aluminium ion. Brackets are not required when only one polyatomic anion is present."
            }
